@title Edge proxy TLS rotation
@subtitle Runbook for rotating the TLS certificates on Redwater Media's 18 edge proxies
@author Platform Security, Owner

kpi Rotate at | 30 days left | CERT_EXPIRY_30D alert
kpi Edge proxies | 18 | 1 canary + 17
kpi Hostnames | 12 | per certificate
kpi Escalate at | 2% handshake errors | any proxy

!!! Never delete the old private key before step 6. Until the old certificate is revoked, that key is the only way back.

# When to rotate
Rotate when a certificate has **30 days or fewer** left. The `CERT_EXPIRY_30D` alert tells you when this happens. Each certificate covers 12 hostnames.

# Before you start
- [ ] The change record is open.
- [ ] No deploy is running: check #deploys.
- [ ] The internal CA is healthy.

# Rotation steps
1. **Confirm expiry.**
-- 1. Run `openssl x509 -enddate -noout` on the live certificate.
-- 2. Compare the date with the alert.
2. **Request the new certificate.**
-- 1. Run `certctl request --profile edge-prod`.
-- 2. Check that the output lists all 12 hostnames.
3. **Stage it on the canary proxy `edge-canary-01` only.**
-- 1. Copy it to `/etc/edge/certs/next`.
-- 2. Reload the proxy.
4. **Verify the canary.**
-- 1. `openssl s_client -connect edge-canary-01:443` shows the new expiry date.
-- 2. Handshake errors stay under 0.1% for 20 minutes.
5. **Roll out to the other 17 proxies** in batches of 3, waiting 5 minutes between batches.
6. **Revoke the old certificate** after 48 hours and record it in the change record.

!! Do not delete the old private key at any point before step 6.

# Rollback
The previous certificate stays in `/etc/edge/certs/previous` for 7 days.
1. Copy it back into place.
2. Reload the proxy.

# Escalation
If any proxy shows **more than 2% handshake errors**:
- Page Platform Security.
- Post in #platform-sec.
